# 8. Key Product Hypotheses

These are hypotheses to validate, not assumptions.

### H1 — Fixing a match is a meaningful pain

Players regularly want to play but fail to convert that intention into a match because finding and coordinating with the right person is difficult.

### H2 — Players already have latent opponent pools

Most active players have several people they would happily play with, but this information currently lives in their memory and WhatsApp history.

### H3 — Multi-person coordination materially reduces friction

Players would prefer one match opportunity sent to several suitable people over sequentially messaging individuals and manually managing responses.

### H4 — Existing networks are the strongest initial supply

Players are more likely to play with people they already know or people connected to someone they trust.

### H5 — Mutual availability can create matches

If players can signal when and where they are available, the product can proactively identify compatible opportunities that would otherwise remain invisible.

### H6 — Good matches are more valuable than generic matches

Players care about compatibility across level, availability, location, format, and social fit.

### H7 — Level compatibility increases match conversion

Players are more willing to play when they understand whether the opponent is approximately their level.

### H8 — Objective progression increases motivation

Seeing improvement in rating and playing history can encourage players to play more frequently.

### H9 — Social visibility creates additional playing opportunities

Seeing what people in one's tennis network are doing can trigger additional matches.

